<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Munger</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Monghyr, Mudgagiri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Munger, formerly Monghyr, is a city on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it was an important centre of the Pala Empire, where royal grants were issued from a victory camp, and near it the Pratihara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagabhata-ii">Nagabhata II</a> defeated the Pala king Dharmapala in the early ninth century. In the 1760s the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a> made it his capital, and it was later known for its fort and gun-making.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/munger.jpg" alt="Munger" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rkrjmp · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_of_Munger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
